ewton}$). Within general relativity, the $L_{
ewton}$-dependent wide binary acceleration receives corrections from a different long-range gravitational effect; as a result, the inner body's acceleration becomes largely independent of the outer body's acceleration, a pattern clearly absent in the observations. In contrast, most alternative theories predict correlative linkages that do not exist purely below $L_{
ewton}$ between the inner and outer accelerations; any validity for the experiment therefore depends on $L_{
ewton}$ staying below the minimum orbital separation. Additionally, because the assumptions of the test are not satisfied by data from the current sample of wide binaries—even if $L_{
ewton}$ were extremely small—the study fails to validly constrain this scale directly. While a follow-up test using data from an expanded sample could potentially determine if the scale is simply sufficiently small, valid limits could be achieved by a successful test design that explicitly incorporates the predicted connections between inner and outer accelerations if $L_{
ewton}$ were large.

